Automated Detection of Fraudulent Online Transactions Using Behaviour-Based Machine Learning Models 
This project focuses on detecting fraudulent online payment transactions using Machine Learning techniques. The dataset was analyzed, preprocessed, and used to train multiple classification models including Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost to identify suspicious financial transactions with high accuracy.

Features
Data preprocessing and cleaning
Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Fraud transaction visualization
Feature engineering using categorical encoding
Machine Learning model training and evaluation
Comparison of multiple classification algorithms
Confusion Matrix visualization for model performance
Technologies Used
Python
Pandas
NumPy
Matplotlib
Seaborn
Scikit-learn
XGBoost
Models Implemented
Logistic Regression
Random Forest Classifier
XGBoost Classifier
Results

The XGBoost Classifier achieved the best performance with approximately 99.4% validation accuracy, outperforming the other models in detecting fraudulent transactions.

Dataset Information

The dataset contains transaction details such as:

Transaction type
Transaction amount
Sender and receiver balances
Fraud labels
Project Workflow
Importing and understanding the dataset
Data preprocessing and handling missing values
Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Feature encoding and selection
Splitting data into training and testing sets
Model training and evaluation
Fraud prediction and visualization
Conclusion

This project demonstrates how Machine Learning can be effectively applied to detect online payment fraud and improve transaction security. Among all models tested, XGBoost provided the highest accuracy and best fraud detection capability.
